{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":2,"output":1375,"cacheRead":10284,"cacheWrite":404,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":12065,"cost":{"input":0.00002,"output":0.06875,"cacheRead":0.010284,"cacheWrite":0.00505,"total":0.08410400000000001}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_end","contentIndex":1,"toolCall":{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_014efXcj5ME3PXC4ZjVodGQY","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-projector/INCIDENT.md","content":"# Event projector incident\n\nStatus: resolved\n\n## Impact\n\n- One temporary persistence rejection permanently broke the projector: every\n  later `project` call for any trade rejected with the same original error.\n- All trades shared one global queue, so a slow or stalled persistence call for\n  one trade blocked unrelated trades.\n- State was committed to the in-memory map before `applyToStore` succeeded, so\n  a failed persist still mutated the snapshot, leaving the store and the\n  projector inconsistent.\n- No version, ordering, terminal-state, or payload validation existed, so\n  gaps, duplicates, post-cancel events, and malformed events were silently\n  applied.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThree defects in `createEventProjector`:\n\n1. **Poisoned chain.** `pending = pending.then(...)` reused the rejected\n   promise as the tail of the queue. Once any handler threw, every subsequent\n   `.then` short-circuited with the old rejection forever.\n2. **Global serialization.** A single `pending` promise queued all trades\n   together instead of maintaining one queue per `tradeId`.\n3. **Commit before persist.** `states.set(...)` ran before `await\n   applyToStore(...)`, so a persistence failure still changed observable\n   state; there was also no validation of versions, event types, payload\n   fields, or terminal (cancelled) status.\n\n## Fix\n\n- Replaced the global queue with a per-trade queue map. Each trade's chain is\n  extended via `previous.catch(() => {}).then(...)` so a rejected event is\n  surfaced to its caller but never poisons the queue tail, and the stored tail\n  is itself swallowed (`run.catch(() => {})`).\n- Moved the `states.set` commit to after `applyToStore` resolves; a rejection\n  leaves state untouched.\n- Added full validation: first event must be `CREATED` at version 1; later\n  events must be exactly `current.version + 1`; duplicates/stale versions\n  return the current snapshot without persisting; gaps, repeated `CREATED`,\n  and events after `CANCELLED` reject with `RangeError`; malformed events\n  (bad type, non-integer version, invalid `notional`/`owner`) reject with\n  `TypeError` before touching the queue or the store.\n- Isolation: the incoming event is `structuredClone`d before queueing, and all\n  states passed to the store callback, returned from `project`, or read via\n  `getSnapshot` are deep copies.\n\n## Regression coverage\n\n`student-tests.mjs` adds five tests:\n\n1. Different trades progress independently when one trade's persistence is\n   stalled (per-trade concurrency).\n2. Duplicate/stale versions return the snapshot without re-persisting.\n3. Version gaps, invalid first events, and events after `CANCELLED` reject\n   without changing state.\n4. Malformed events (`null`, unsupported type, negative/infinite notional,\n   empty owner, non-integer version) reject with `TypeError` and never reach\n   the store.\n5. Mutating inputs, callback arguments, returned states, or snapshots does not\n   affect internal state.\n\nThe committed test file additionally covers per-trade serialization and\nrecovery after a temporary persistence rejection.\n\n## Remaining risk\n\n- State is in-memory only; a process restart loses projections and there is no\n  rehydration from the store.\n- `applyToStore` and the state commit are not atomic: if the process dies\n  after persist but before commit, a replay of the same event would be\n  persisted twice (the store must be idempotent on `(tradeId, version)`).\n- Events must be structured-cloneable; exotic payloads (functions, proxies)\n  are rejected with `TypeError` by design.\n- Per-trade queue entries are never garbage-collected, so a very large number\n  of distinct trade IDs grows the maps unboundedly.\n"}}}}
